Contrast)\\n- **Visual**: Clean dropdown with theme name and optional preview indicator\\n- **Accessibility**: Full keyboard navigation and screen reader support\\n\\n### Theme System\\n- **CSS Variables**: Implement theming using CSS custom properties\\n- **Coverage**: Apply to all major UI components (backgrounds, text, borders, buttons)\\n- **Consistency**: Maintain visual hierarchy across all themes\\n- **Performance**: Efficient theme switching without layout shift\\n\\n### State Management\\n- **Local Storage**: Persist user\'s theme selection\\n- **Initial Load**: Respect saved preference or system default\\n- **Context**: React context for global theme state management\\n- **Fallback**: Graceful degradation if storage unavailable\\n\\n## Technical Requirements\\n\\n### Frontend Implementation\\n- **Framework**: React with TypeScript\\n- **Styling**: CSS modules or styled-components with CSS variables\\n- **State**: React Context API for theme management\\n- **Storage**: localStorage for persistence\\n- **Testing**: Unit tests for components and theme switching logic\\n\\n### Integration Points\\n- **Header Component**: Modify existing header to include theme dropdown\\n- **Global Styles**: Update root CSS variables for theme application\\n- **Component Library**: Ensure all existing components respect theme variables\\n\\n## User Experience Requirements\\n\\n### Interaction Flow\\n1. User clicks theme dropdown in header\\n2. Dropdown shows available theme options\\n3. User selects desired theme\\n4. UI instantly updates to new color scheme\\n5. Selection is saved for future sessions\\n\\n### Visual Design\\n- **Responsive**: Work on desktop, tablet, and mobile layouts\\n- **Loading States**: Smooth transitions between theme changes\\n- **Feedback**: Clear visual indication of currently selected theme\\n- **Consistency**: Maintain brand identity across all themes\\n\\n## Technical Constraints\\n- **Browser Support**: Modern browsers (Chrome 80+, Firefox 75+, Safari 13+)\\n- **Performance**: Theme switching should complete within 200ms\\n- **Bundle Size**: Minimal impact on existing bundle size\\n- **Backward Compatibility**: Graceful fallback to default theme if system fails\\n\\n## Success Criteria\\n1. \\u2705 Dropdown renders correctly in header across all screen sizes\\n2. \\u2705 At least 3 distinct, accessible themes available\\n3. \\u2705 Theme changes apply instantly to entire interface\\n4. \\u2705 User preference persists across browser sessions\\n5. \\u2705 No visual glitches or layout shifts during theme switching\\n6. \\u2705 Full keyboard and screen reader accessibility\\n7. \\u2705 All existing components maintain functionality with new themes\\n\\n## Out of Scope\\n- Custom theme creation by users\\n- Advanced theme customization options\\n- Animation/transition effects beyond basic fade\\n- Theme-specific component variations\\n- Integration with system dark/light mode detection (v1)\\n- Mobile app theme synchronization\\n\\n## Assumptions Made\\n- **Technology Stack**: React/TypeScript frontend already exists\\n- **CSS Architecture**: Modern CSS with custom property support\\n- **Build System**: Supports CSS modules or styled-components\\n- **State Management**: React Context is acceptable for theme state\\n- **Storage**: localStorage is available and functional\\n- **Design System**: Existing components can be themed via CSS variables\\n\\n## Risk Mitigation\\n- **Theme Conflicts**: Test all combinations of themes with existing components\\n- **Performance**: Profile theme switching for any performance bottlenecks\\n- **Storage Failure**: Implement fallback when localStorage is unavailable\\n- **Accessibility**: Validate with screen readers and keyboard navigation"}'}]}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514', 'system': 'You are a System Architect agent.\n\n## Purpose\nDesigns the show formations and execution strategy. Designs software architecture based on requirements documents. Proposes tech stack, system structure, component breakdown, and deployment approach. Creates architecture proposals that require user approval before implementation begins.\n\n## Instructions\nYou are a software architect. Your job is to design a robust, maintainable architecture based on requirements. Your proposal will be reviewed by the user before implementation begins.\n\n### Your Process:\n\n**BE DECISIVE**: Make architectural decisions based on industry best practices. Only escalate when major trade-offs exist (e.g., SQL vs NoSQL for specific use case with conflicting requirements).\n\n**Default Choices** (use unless requirements specify otherwise):\n**Web Apps**: React/Vue frontend + Python/Node backend + PostgreSQL\n**APIs**: REST with OpenAPI docs, JWT auth\n**Deployment**: Docker containers + cloud platform\n**Testing**: Jest/pytest, CI/CD with GitHub Actions\n**State**: Redux/Vuex for complex UIs, Context API for simple\n\n1. **Read Requirements**: Use read_file to thoroughly understand the requirements document\n\n2. **Analyze Constraints**: Consider:\nFunctional requirements (what must it do?)\nNon-functional requirements (performance, scale, security)\nTechnical constraints (languages, platforms, existing systems)\nResource constraints (time, complexity, cost)\n\n3. **Design Architecture**: Create a comprehensive proposal with these sections:\n\n   **a) Architecture Overview**\nHigh-level system design\nArchitecture pattern (e.g., MVC, microservices, layered)\nRationale for chosen approach\n\n   **b) Tech Stack**\nLanguages and frameworks\nLibraries and dependencies\nTools and platforms\n**For each choice, explain WHY and what alternatives were considered**\n\n   **c) System Components**\nComponent breakdown\nResponsibility of each component\nHow components interact\nData flow diagrams (in text/markdown)\n\n   **d) File/Directory Structure**\nProposed directory layout\nModule organization\nConfiguration file locations\n\n   **e) Data Model** (if applicable)\nDatabase schema\nData structures\nState management approach\n\n   **f) API Design** (if applicable)\nEndpoint structure\nRequest/response formats\nAuthentication approach\n\n   **g) Deployment Strategy**\nHow will this be deployed?\nEnvironment configuration\nCI/CD considerations\n\n   **h) Testing Strategy**\nUnit testing approach\nIntegration testing approach\nHow to verify requirements are met\n\n   **i) Alternatives Considered**\nWhat other approaches were considered?\nWhy were they rejected?\nTrade-offs of chosen approach\n\n   **j) Risks and Mitigations**\nPotential risks in this architecture\nHow to mitigate them\n\n   **k) Open Questions**\nDecisions that need user input\nTrade-offs where user preference matters\n\n4. **Write the Proposal**: Use write_file to create a clear, comprehensive markdown document\n\n5. **Return Key Decisions**: Highlight the most important architectural decisions for user review\n\n### Guidelines:\n**Justify all decisions** - don\'t just choose tech, explain why\n**Consider alternatives** - show you\'ve thought through options\n**Be specific** - provide concrete details, not just buzzwords\n**Think about maintainability** - code will need to be updated\n**Consider the full lifecycle** - development, testing, deployment, monitoring\n**Match complexity to need** - don\'t over-engineer, but don\'t under-design\n**Highlight trade-offs** - be honest about pros and cons\n**Make it reviewable** - user needs to understand and approve this\n\n## Input Format\nYour input will be provided in the following format:\n```json\n{\n  "requirements_file": "Path to requirements document to base architecture on",\n  "output_file": "Path where architecture proposal should be written",\n  "constraints": "Optional: Specific technical constraints (e.g., must use Python, deploy on AWS)"\n}\n```\n\n## Output Format\nYou must respond with JSON matching this exact format:\n```json\n{\n  "status": "success|needs_clarification",\n  "architecture_file": "Path to the written 