{"timestamp": "2026-01-15 17:23:48", "level": "DEBUG", "module": "anthropic._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/v1/messages', 'timeout': Timeout(connect=5.0, read=600, write=600, pool=600), 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-84695c67-8fdc-4935-b874-8b27063a72f3', 'json_data': {'max_tokens': 4096, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Please complete the following task:\n\n{\n  "requirements_file": "/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/requirements.md",\n  "output_file": "/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md",\n  "project_name": "fix_recovered_jobs_continuation"\n}\n\nRemember to respond with valid JSON matching the expected output format.\n'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_01UeEM1gBZpB8ePUFNezLYhP', 'name': 'read_file', 'input': {'file_path': '/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/requirements.md'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_01UeEM1gBZpB8ePUFNezLYhP', 'content': '{"success": true, "content": "# UI Color Scheme Dropdown - Requirements\\n\\n## Project Vision\\nImplement a dropdown component in the header that allows users to dynamically change the UI color scheme/theme of the ensemble interface without requiring a page refresh.\\n\\n## Core Objectives\\n1. **Theme Selection**: Enable users to choose from multiple predefined color schemes\\n2. **Persistent State**: Store user\'s theme preference across browser sessions\\n3. **Dynamic Application**: Apply theme changes instantly without page reload\\n4. **Header Integration**: Seamlessly integrate dropdown into existing header component\\n\\n## Functional Requirements\\n\\n### Theme Dropdown Component\\n- **Location**: Position in the header navigation area\\n- **Options**: Minimum 3 theme options (Light, Dark, High Contrast)\\n- **Visual**: Clean dropdown with theme name and optional preview indicator\\n- **Accessibility**: Full keyboard navigation and screen reader support\\n\\n### Theme System\\n- **CSS Variables**: Implement theming using CSS custom properties\\n- **Coverage**: Apply to all major UI components (backgrounds, text, borders, buttons)\\n- **Consistency**: Maintain visual hierarchy across all themes\\n- **Performance**: Efficient theme switching without layout shift\\n\\n### State Management\\n- **Local Storage**: Persist user\'s theme selection\\n- **Initial Load**: Respect saved preference or system default\\n- **Context**: React context for global theme state management\\n- **Fallback**: Graceful degradation if storage unavailable\\n\\n## Technical Requirements\\n\\n### Frontend Implementation\\n- **Framework**: React with TypeScript\\n- **Styling**: CSS modules or styled-components with CSS variables\\n- **State**: React Context API for theme management\\n- **Storage**: localStorage for persistence\\n- **Testing**: Unit tests for components and theme switching logic\\n\\n### Integration Points\\n- **Header Component**: Modify existing header to include theme dropdown\\n- **Global Styles**: Update root CSS variables for theme application\\n- **Component Library**: Ensure all existing components respect theme variables\\n\\n## User Experience Requirements\\n\\n### Interaction Flow\\n1. User clicks theme dropdown in header\\n2. Dropdown shows available theme options\\n3. User selects desired theme\\n4. UI instantly updates to new color scheme\\n5. Selection is saved for future sessions\\n\\n### Visual Design\\n- **Responsive**: Work on desktop, tablet, and mobile layouts\\n- **Loading States**: Smooth transitions between theme changes\\n- **Feedback**: Clear visual indication of currently selected theme\\n- **Consistency**: Maintain brand identity across all themes\\n\\n## Technical Constraints\\n- **Browser Support**: Modern browsers (Chrome 80+, Firefox 75+, Safari 13+)\\n- **Performance**: Theme switching should complete within 200ms\\n- **Bundle Size**: Minimal impact on existing bundle size\\n- **Backward Compatibility**: Graceful fallback to default theme if system fails\\n\\n## Success Criteria\\n1. \\u2705 Dropdown renders correctly in header across all screen sizes\\n2. \\u2705 At least 3 distinct, accessible themes available\\n3. \\u2705 Theme changes apply instantly to entire interface\\n4. \\u2705 User preference persists across browser sessions\\n5. \\u2705 No visual glitches or layout shifts during theme switching\\n6. \\u2705 Full keyboard and screen reader accessibility\\n7. \\u2705 All existing components maintain functionality with new themes\\n\\n## Out of Scope\\n- Custom theme creation by users\\n- Advanced theme customization options\\n- Animation/transition effects beyond basic fade\\n- Theme-specific component variations\\n- Integration with system dark/light mode detection (v1)\\n- Mobile app theme synchronization\\n\\n## Assumptions Made\\n- **Technology Stack**: React/TypeScript frontend already exists\\n- **CSS Architecture**: Modern CSS with custom property support\\n- **Build System**: Supports CSS modules or styled-components\\n- **State Management**: React Context is acceptable for theme state\\n- **Storage**: localStorage is available and functional\\n- **Design System**: Existing components can be themed via CSS variables\\n\\n## Risk Mitigation\\n- **Theme Conflicts**: Test all combinations of themes with existing components\\n- **Performance**: Profile theme switching for any performance bottlenecks\\n- **Storage Failure**: Implement fallback when localStorage is unavailable\\n- **Accessibility**: Validate with screen readers and keyboard navigation"}'}]}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_014ewjU6CKZfbhEK34nUwCJ8', 'name': 'write_file', 'input': {'file_path': '/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md', 'content': '# UI Color Scheme Dropdown - Architecture Proposal\n\n## Architecture Overview\n\nThis architecture implements a **Theme-Driven Component Pattern** using React Context and CSS Custom Properties. The design follows a **layered architecture** with clear separation between:\n- Theme state management (Context layer)\n- Theme application (CSS variable layer)\n- UI components (Component layer)\n- Persistence (Storage layer)\n\n**Rationale**: This approach provides clean separation of concerns, minimal performance overhead, and excellent maintainability. The CSS custom properties pattern is industry standard for theming and offers instant visual updates without re-rendering components.\n\n## Tech Stack\n\n### Core Technologies\n- **React 18+** with TypeScript - Existing frontend framework\n- **CSS Modules** with CSS Custom Properties - Theme implementation\n- **React Context API** - Global theme state management\n- **localStorage** - Theme preference persistence\n\n### Specific Libraries\n- **react**: ^18.0.0 (existing)\n- **typescript**: ^4.9.0+ (existing)\n- **@types/react**: ^18.0.0 (existing)\n\n**Rationale for CSS Modules + Custom Properties**:\n- **Considered**: styled-components, emotion, vanilla CSS\n- **Chosen**: CSS Modules + Custom Properties for performance and simplicity\n- **Why**: CSS variables provide instant theme switching without JavaScript re-renders, smaller bundle size than CSS-in-JS solutions, and better browser performance\n\n**Rationale for React Context**:\n- **Considered**: Redux, Zustand, local state\n- **Chosen**: React Context API\n- **Why**: Theme state is global but simple (current theme + setter), Context API is sufficient and avoids external dependencies\n\n## System Components\n\n### 1. ThemeProvider (`src/components/theme/ThemeProvider.tsx`)\n**Responsibility**: Global theme state management and persistence\n- Manages current theme state\n- Handles localStorage persistence\n- Provides theme context to app\n- Applies theme CSS variables to document root\n\n### 2. ThemeDropdown (`src/components/theme/ThemeDropdown.tsx`)\n**Responsibility**: User interface for theme selection\n- Renders dropdown in header\n- Handles user theme selection\n- Shows current theme selection\n- Accessible dropdown implementation\n\n### 3. useTheme Hook (`src/hooks/useTheme.ts`)\n**Responsibility**: Theme context consumption interface\n- Provides theme state and setter\n- Encapsulates context usage\n- Type-safe theme operations\n\n### 4. Theme Definitions (`src/styles/themes.ts`)\n**Responsibility**: Theme configuration and CSS variable mapping\n- Defines available themes\n- Maps theme names to CSS variable values\n- Provides theme metadata (labels, descriptions)\n\n### 5. CSS Variable System (`src/styles/variables.css`)\n**Responsibility**: Global CSS custom properties\n- Defines all themeable CSS variables\n- Sets default values\n- Documents variable usage\n\n## Component Interaction Flow\n\n```\nUser Interaction → ThemeDropdown → useTheme Hook → ThemeProvider\n                                                         ↓\nThemeProvider → document.documentElement.style → CSS Variables\n                                                         ↓\nCSS Variables → All Styled Components → Visual Update\n```\n\n## File/Directory Structure\n\n```\nsrc/\n├── components/\n│   ├── theme/\n│   │   ├── ThemeProvider.tsx          # Global theme context provider\n│   │   ├── ThemeDropdown.tsx          # Dropdown UI component\n│   │   └── __tests__/\n│   │       ├── ThemeProvider.test.tsx\n│   │       └── ThemeDropdown.test.tsx\n│   └── header/\n│       └── Header.tsx                 # Modified to include ThemeDropdown\n├── hooks/\n│   ├── useTheme.ts                    # Theme context hook\n│   └── __tests__/\n│       └── useTheme.test.ts\n├── styles/\n│   ├── themes.ts                      # Theme definitions and config\n│   ├── variables.css                  # CSS custom properties\n│   └── themes/\n│       ├── light.css                  # Light theme variables\n│       ├── dark.css                   # Dark theme variables\n│       └── high-contrast.css          # High contrast theme variables\n├── types/\n│   └── theme.types.ts                 # TypeScript type definitions\n└── utils/\n    ├── storage.ts                     # localStorage utilities\n    └── __tests__/\n        └── storage.test.ts\n```\n\n## Data Model\n\n### Theme State\n```typescript\ninterface ThemeContextType {\n  currentTheme: ThemeName;\n  setTheme: (theme: ThemeName) => void;\n  availableThemes: Theme[];\n}\n\ninterface Theme {\n  name: ThemeName;\n  label: string;\n  description?: string;\n  cssVariables: Record<string, string>;\n}\n\ntype ThemeName = \'light\' | \'dark\' | \'high-contrast\';\n```\n\n### CSS Variable Structure\n```css\n:root {\n  /* Background colors */\n  --color-bg-primary: #ffffff;\n  --color-bg-secondary: #f8f9fa;\n  --color-bg-accent: #e9ecef;\n  \n  /* Text colors */\n  --color-text-primary: #212529;\n  --color-text-secondary: #6c757d;\n  --color-text-inverse: #ffffff;\n  \n  /* Border colors */\n  --color-border-primary: #dee2e6;\n  --color-border-secondary: #e9ecef;\n  \n  /* Interactive colors */\n  --color-interactive-primary: #0d6efd;\n  --color-interactive-hover: #0b5ed7;\n  --color-interactive-disabled: #6c757d;\n}\n```\n\n## API Design\n\n### Theme Context API\n```typescript\n// Theme provider usage\n<ThemeProvider defaultTheme="light">\n  <App />\n</ThemeProvider>\n\n// Theme consumption\nconst { currentTheme, setTheme, availableThemes } = useTheme();\n\n// Theme switching\nsetTheme(\'dark\');\n```\n\n### Storage API\n```typescript\n// Save theme preference\nsaveThemePreference(theme: ThemeName): void\n\n// Load theme preference\nloadThemePreference(): ThemeName | null\n\n// Check storage availability\nisStorageAvailable(): boolean\n```\n\n## Deployment Strategy\n\n### Development Setup\n1. Install dependencies (none additional required)\n2. Add new components and styles to existing build process\n3. Import ThemeProvider in App.tsx root component\n\n### Build Process Integration\n- CSS modules handled by existing Webpack/Vite configuration\n- TypeScript compilation includes new type definitions\n- No additional build steps required\n\n### Testing Environment\n- Unit tests run with existing Jest configuration\n- CSS variable mocking for theme testing\n- localStorage mocking for persistence testing\n\n## Testing Strategy\n\n### Unit Testing\n```typescript\n// ThemeProvider tests\n- ✅ Provides theme context correctly\n- ✅ Applies CSS variables to document\n- ✅ Persists theme selection to localStorage\n- ✅ Loads saved theme on initialization\n- ✅ Falls back to default when localStorage unavailable\n\n// ThemeDropdown tests\n- ✅ Renders all available themes\n- ✅ Shows current theme selection\n- ✅ Calls setTheme on selection\n- ✅ Keyboard navigation works\n- ✅ Screen reader accessibility\n\n// useTheme hook tests\n- ✅ Returns current theme state\n- ✅ Throws error when used outside provider\n- ✅ Updates when theme changes\n```\n\n### Integration Testing\n```typescript\n// End-to-end theme switching\n- ✅ Theme changes apply to all components\n- ✅ No visual glitches during transition\n- ✅ Performance within 200ms requirement\n- ✅ Persistence across page reloads\n```\n\n### Accessibility Testing\n- Screen reader compatibility (NVDA, JAWS)\n- Keyboard navigation (Tab, Enter, Arrow keys)\n- Color contrast validation for all themes\n- Focus management in dropdown\n\n## Alternatives Considered\n\n### 1. CSS-in-JS Solutions (styled-components, emotion)\n**Rejected because**:\n- Larger bundle size impact\n- Runtime performance overhead for theme switching\n- More complex integration with existing CSS\n\n### 2. Redux for State Management\n**Rejected because**:\n- Overkill for simple theme state\n- Additional dependency and boilerplate\n- React Context sufficient for this use case\n\n### 3. Vanilla CSS with Class Switching\n**Rejected because**:\n- Less flexible than CSS variables\n- Requires more CSS duplication\n- Harder to maintain consistent theming\n\n### 4. SCSS/Sass Variables\n**Rejected because**:\n- Cannot be changed at runtime\n- Would require build-time theme generation\n- Less dynamic than CSS custom properties\n\n## Risks and Mitigations\n\n### Risk: Browser Support for CSS Variables\n**Mitigation**: Graceful fallback with default styles, progressive enhancement\n\n### Risk: localStorage Unavailable\n**Mitigation**: Storage availability check, fallback to session state and default theme\n\n### Risk: Performance Impact\n**Mitigation**: Minimal CSS variable usage, batch DOM updates, performance monitoring\n\n### Risk: Theme Variable Conflicts\n**Mitigation**: Namespaced variable names, comprehensive testing across all components\n\n### Risk: Accessibility Issues\n**Mitigation**: Comprehensive accessibility testing, semantic HTML, ARIA attributes\n\n## Open Questions for Review\n\n1. **Theme Options**: Are Light, Dark, and High Contrast sufficient, or do you want additional themes?\n\n2. **Theme Scope**: Should themes affect only colors, or include typography, spacing, and other design tokens?\n\n3. **Default Theme**: Should the default theme be Light, or should it detect system preference?\n\n4. **Header Position**: Is the current header the correct location, or would you prefer the dropdown elsewhere?\n\n5. **Theme Preview**: Do you want preview indicators in the dropdown (color swatches, thumbnails)?\n\n## Implementation Priority\n\n### Phase 1: Core Infrastructure\n1. Theme types and definitions\n2. ThemeProvider and Context\n3. CSS variable system\n4. Basic theme switching\n\n### Phase 2: UI Components\n1. ThemeDropdown component\n2. Header integration\n3. Accessibility features\n4. Visual polish\n\n### Phase 3: Persistence & Testing\n1. localStorage integration\n2. Comprehensive test suite\n3. Performance optimization\n4. Cross-browser testing\n\nThis architecture provides a solid foundation for theme switching while maintaining excellent performance and user experience. The modular design allows for easy extension and maintenance as the application evolves.'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_014ewjU6CKZfbhEK34nUwCJ8', 'content': '{"success": true, "message": "File written successfully to /Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md (backup: /Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture_20260115_172348.md)", "backup_path": "/Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture_20260115_172348.md", "was_overwrite": true}'}]}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514', 'system': 'You are a System Architect agent.\n\n## Purpose\nDesigns the show formations and execution strategy. Designs software architecture based on requirements documents. Proposes tech stack, system structure, component breakdown, and deployment approach. Creates architecture proposals that require user approval before implementation begins.\n\n## Instructions\nYou are a software architect. Your job is to design a robust, maintainable architecture based on requirements. Your proposal will be reviewed by the user before implementation begins.\n\n### Your Process:\n\n**BE DECISIVE**: Make architectural decisions based on industry best practices. Only escalate when major trade-offs exist (e.g., SQL vs NoSQL for specific use case with conflicting requirements).\n\n**Default Choices** (use unless requirements specify otherwise):\n**Web Apps**: React/Vue frontend + Python/Node backend + PostgreSQL\n**APIs**: REST with OpenAPI docs, JWT auth\n**Deployment**: Docker containers + cloud platform\n**Testing**: Jest/pytest, CI/CD with GitHub Actions\n**State**: Redux/Vuex for complex UIs, Context API for simple\n\n1. **Read Requirements**: Use read_file to thoroughly understand the requirements document\n\n2. **Analyze Constraints**: Consider:\nFunctional requirements (what must it do?)\nNon-functional requirements (performance, scale, security)\nTechnical constraints (languages, platforms, existing systems)\nResource constraints (time, complexity, cost)\n\n3. **Design Architecture**: Create a comprehensive proposal with these sections:\n\n   **a) Architecture Overview**\nHigh-level system design\nArchitecture pattern (e.g., MVC, microservices, layered)\nRationale for chosen approach\n\n   **b) Tech Stack**\nLanguages and frameworks\nLibraries and dependencies\nTools and platforms\n**For each choice, explain WHY and what alternatives were considered**\n\n   **c) System Components**\nComponent breakdown\nResponsibility of each component\nHow components interact\nData flow diagrams (in text/markdown)\n\n   **d) File/Directory Structure**\nProposed directory layout\nModule organization\nConfiguration file locations\n\n   **e) Data Model** (if applicable)\nDatabase schema\nData structures\nState management approach\n\n   **f) API Design** (if applicable)\nEndpoint structure\nRequest/response formats\nAuthentication approach\n\n   **g) Deployment Strategy**\nHow will this be deployed?\nEnvironment configuration\nCI/CD considerations\n\n   **h) Testing Strategy**\nUnit testing approach\nIntegration testing approach\nHow to verify requirements are met\n\n   **i) Alternatives Considered**\nWhat other approaches were considered?\nWhy were they rejected?\nTrade-offs of chosen approach\n\n   **j) Risks and Mitigations**\nPotential risks in this architecture\nHow to mitigate them\n\n   **k) Open Questions**\nDecisions that need user input\nTrade-offs where user preference matters\n\n4. **Write the Proposal**: Use write_file to create a clear, comprehensive markdown document\n\n5. **Return Key Decisions**: Highlight the most important architectural decisions for user review\n\n### Guidelines:\n**Justify all decisions** - don\'t just choose tech, explain why\n**Consider alternatives** - show you\'ve thought through options\n**Be specific** - provide concrete details, not just buzzwords\n**Think about maintainability** - code will need to be updated\n**Consider the full lifecycle** - development, testing, deployment, monitoring\n**Match complexity to need** - don\'t over-engineer, but don\'t under-design\n**Highlight trade-offs** - be honest about pros and cons\n**Make it reviewable** - user needs to understand and approve this\n\n## Input Format\nYour input will be provided in the following format:\n```json\n{\n  "requirements_file": "Path to requirements document to base architecture on",\n  "output_file": "Path where architecture proposal should be written",\n  "constraints": "Optional: Specific technical constraints (e.g., must use Python, deploy on AWS)"\n}\n```\n\n## Output Format\nYou must respond with JSON matching this exact format:\n```json\n{\n  "status": "success|needs_clarification",\n  "architecture_file": "Path to the written architecture proposal",\n  "message": "Summary of architectural approach",\n  "key_decisions": ["List of major architectural decisions for user review"],\n  "self_analysis": "string - REQUIRED: Your performance analysis (2-4 sentences, see Self-Improvement Directive)"\n}\n```\n\n## When to Request Clarification\nRequest clarification when:\n\n\n## Termination Conditions\nYou are done when:\n- Complete architecture proposal has been written\n- Tech stack is justified with trade-offs explained\n- File structure and component breakdown are defined\n- Data flow is documented\n- Alternative approaches are considered\n\nCRITICAL: Your response must be valid JSON matching the Output Format exactly.\n\n## Your Performance History\n- Success rate: 100.0%\n- Average iterations per task: 3.6\n\n**Tips for this run:**\n- Great work! Maintain your high performance standards.\n\n**Learnings from your recent runs:**\n- Successfully analyzed a simple content addition requirement and designed an appropriately minimal architecture. Applied the Data Extension Pattern correctly for low-risk additive changes, provided com...\n- Successfully analyzed a content addition requirement and designed an appropriately minimal architecture using the Data Extension Pattern. The proposal balances D&D authenticity with technical pragmati...\n', 'tools': [{'name': 'write_file', 'description': 'Write content to a file, creating parent directories if needed. Automatically backs up existing files.', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to write'}, 'content': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Content to write to the file'}}, 'required': ['file_path', 'content']}}, {'name': 'read_file', 'description': 'Read content from a file', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to read'}}, 'required': ['file_path']}}, {'name': 'run_command', 'description': 'Execute a shell command and return the output', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'command': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The shell command to execute'}, 'working_directory': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Optional working directory for command execution'}}, 'required': ['command']}}, {'name': 'git_commit', 'description': 'Commit changes to the git repository with a descriptive message', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Commit message describing the changes'}, 'files': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}, 'description': 'Optional list of specific files to commit. If not provided, commits all modified files.'}}, 'required': ['message']}}, {'name': 'git_branch', 'description': 'Create, switch, list, or delete git branches for coordinated development', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['create', 'switch', 'list', 'delete', 'current'], 'description': 'Action to perform: create new branch, switch to existing, list all, delete, or get current'}, 'branch_name': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Name of the branch (for create, switch, delete)'}, 'from_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Base branch to create from (for create, defaults to current branch)'}, 'force': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Force the operation (for delete unmerged branches)', 'default': False}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'git_merge', 'description': 'Merge branches or create pull requests for code review', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['merge', 'preview', 'abort'], 'description': 'Action: merge branches, preview changes, or abort an in-progress merge'}, 'source_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Branch to merge from'}, 'target_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Branch to merge into (defaults to current branch)'}, 'strategy': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['merge', 'squash', 'rebase'], 'description': 'Merge strategy (default: merge)', 'default': 'merge'}, 'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Custom merge commit message (optional)'}, 'no_ff': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Create merge commit even if fast-forward is possible', 'default': True}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'git_status', 'description': 'Get comprehensive git repository status including branches, changes, and history', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'include_history': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Include recent commit history', 'default': False}, 'history_count': {'type': 'integer', 'description': 'Number of commits to include in history', 'default': 10}}, 'required': []}}, {'name': 'project_tracking', 'description': 'Manage project state, tasks, and notes for the current project', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['create_project', 'add_task', 'update_task', 'add_note', 'get_summary', 'get_next_tasks'], 'description': 'Action to perform'}, 'projec